A||||64||Juiced_V8||superchargers,turbos,nos,blower||||3||
Z||000000||Juiced_V8||10-24-2001||10:04 PM||||i was looking for genral opinions on the benifits\drawbacks of these power adders,i heard that the sc 3800's wastegate is set at 7 psi of boost,is this adjustable ?<BR><P>------------------<BR>86 gt v8 fogger set up sold going to get a simple sniper system with a plate||||reg||3
Z||000001||Turbo88||10-25-2001||12:13 AM||||Ahhh.. my favorite subject <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Here is my thoughts on each. Opinions are like ass holes, everyone has one so take it for what its worth. <P>I love power adders. Who doesn't? Each has their place and does something better than the others due but you pay a price somewhere else... Here goes........... <P>-Juice basicly makes the world perfect from an engine's point of view. Nitrous Oxide is basicly really pure atmospheric air. It is composed of two nitrogen atoms and one oxygen per molcule. That makes it 33% oxygen. As we all know, oxygen is what the engine actually burns with the fuel. The air we (and the engine) breath is closer to 22-25% oxygen. So right away, there is more oxygen to burn per volume of N2O vs air. What really makes it pack a punch is temperature. As the gas leaving the bottle decompresses; it drastically cools. So by temperature, it allows a much higher density than it does at ambiant. So we have more oxygen per weight than air and it is much more dense. Together that gives you a whole lot of oxygen to burn fuel in.<P>The benefit to juice is you cann drive around a very streetable machine and turn it into a fire breathing dragon in the push of a button! It gives the best BANG for the buck by far! <P>The drawback is it will find any weaknesses in your engine and happily shell it for you. Nitrous is so hard on engines because you get this instant increase in combustion chamber presure that no piston engine was originally designed for. Technology has allowed us to produce parts that can withstand the abuse but engines like concurrent powerbands. They don't like making 75hp at 1000 rpm and 175 at 1100. But some can take it. Either use moderate amounts of N2O or build a motor that can handle it. The only other thing I don't like about it is, it will lean your motor out before you or your ecm can add fuel to compensate if you install it wrong or over jet it. <P>-Superchargers and blowers tend to be a term used to describe the same device. Both superchargers and turbochargers do the same thing but their implementations are a little different. Superchargers, being driven directly by the engine avoid the "lag" you might find in a turbo application. The drawback to this is, your boost level is dependant on engine RPM and you will either not make maximum boost until redline or you will waste engine horsepower compressing air you will later vent into the atmosphere before it's burned. With the exception of centrifugal superchargers (like a paxton or vortech), superchargers tend to be less effiecent. Efficency in a compressor like a turbo or supercharger is measured by how much heat it adds to the air charge while it is compressing it. Just like I mentioned above, nitrous cools as it decompresses. The inverse works on the air in that it heats up as it is compressed. If a cooler charge is a more dense charge the a hotter charge is less dense. The cooler the air charge, the more molicules you have in a given area. <P>-Turbochargers are, in my humble opinion, (see above) the best for anything short of an full blown sub 7 second drag car. Here is why.. A well designed turbo kit will have almost null lag. It will come to full boost well before you see redline which means you are going to make more power sooner at the cost of less power on launch. The myth of exhaust adding heat to the intake charge is unfounded. A given volume of air isn't in the turbo long enough for ambient heat to cause any significant effect. Heat comes from compression. <P>For an all out drag car, a turbo means weaker launches and slower 60 foot times than a supercharger you will have a broader power curve and can potentially stay in full boost through out 95% of the run vs 30% or less on a supercharger. <P>For a road race car, you will find none better. With a good blow off valve, your turbo can stay spooled (spinning at high RPM) for almost all of your run. Sinse you are either full throttle or no throttle, you have many benefits. Your turbo will help you scavange your exhaust because the inerta of the turbine will cause it to spin faster than the exhaust is flowing across it, causing a vacuum in the exhaust port. When you reopen the throttle, you have instant full on boost ready to take off. <P>For a street car, *I* think a turbo is a better choice. A turbo engine at part throttle will behave much like a naturally aspirated engine. It will be mild mannered because it isn't underboost and it doesn't have a supercharger to spin. <P>Some myths about turbos...<BR>1. Turbos cause excessive backpressure.<P>Not totally true. Think back to high school physics and the law of conservation of energy. A turbo uses energy already present in the exhaust in the exhaust system to compress air and force it into the engine. A turbo uses the same energy your muffler dissipates to keep your car quiet (some of us <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). In your exhaust system you have hot, fast moving gasses under pressure. Those forces are what spins the turbine. Under some conditions a turbo will cause pressure to stack up in the exhaust port. This mostly occurs when the turbo is coming up to speed. After that, exhaust pressure is allowed to bypass the turbine via a wastegate. While the backpressure issue is valid, there is another force taht counter acts it. The boost it is creating is causing positive pressure at the intake valve. So the negitive effects of backpressure making it harder to get rid of exhaust are countered by the positive pressure of the fresh air being forced into the cylinder. This positive pressure is almost always more than the backpressure so your incoming air charge forces the extra exhaust pressure out. Under part throttle, the effects of either are negligable and under closed throttle (between gears), the turbine pulls exhaust out of the engine. <P>2. Turbos cause extra heat in the engine bay.<P>This is absolutely true. That energy I talked about above it converted to heat and the turbine housing gets VERY hot. You have to consider heat in your turbo placement. <P>3. Turbos heat the incoming air more than superchargers because they are driven by exhaust.<P>This is true but the amount of heat added by the turbine is almost nothing because almost every single compressor housing is aluminum which dissipated heat very well. Also, a given volume of air is in the compressor section for such a short time, it has little chance to gain heat from the compressor walls. Because superchargers are less efficent, they heat the are more on compression. Again the exception is the centrifugal units which are nothing more than a turbo compressor driven by a pully, not an exhaust turbine. <P><BR>There are a ton more of these but I'm too tired to go into the rest of them. for the same reason, my grammer and spelling is probably terrible in this post. I just got back from Newark and I'm running on only a couple hours sleep so please forgive..<P>Hope this helps answer some questions.. I told you it would be long winded!<P>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||roadwarrior||10-25-2001||11:31 AM||||first I'd like to make a correction a blower and a turbo charger aren't the same thing, a blower runs off the engine's belts where as a turbo runs off expanding exhaust gases, <P>- like you said nos is good for many applications because it can be turned on or off, but like everything it has it's downsides.<P>- a turbo is basically a small compressor, and it's basis of opperation is the same as a turbine(hence the name turbo) exhaust gases spin one "propeller" that is connected via a solid shaft thus spining another "prop"<BR>which compresses the intake air, but as we all know engines run best on cold air, and whenever you compress air it heats up, so to get the most power from this mod you are better off also instaling an intercooler.One downside to turbos is you have to idle you engine for a few minutes but shuting it down or you will damage the turbo from insuficient oiling, all oil that goes to the turbo  is pushed there by oil pressure so when you shut down the engine the turbo looses all it's oil. When installing a turbo you should also install a high flow oil pump to get the oil pressure high enough to keep the turbo lubicated.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Screwie||10-25-2001||12:03 PM||||Hmm, he said a blower is the same as a supercharger. But anyway, you said that you need to idle your engine before shutting it down. Is that because the turbine is still spinning? And so a supercharger wouldn't need that as it is belt driven? Or am I totally off here?||||reg||1
Z||000004||watts||10-25-2001||12:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Screwie:<BR><B>you said that you need to idle your engine before shutting it down. Is that because the turbine is still spinning? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The thing is...  the turbo can spin at up to 100,000 RPM (depending on model, bla bla).  If say you're out booting around and have thing spooled way up, then quicky shut the engine off, you also shut off the oil flow.  Screeeech, no lube to the turbo bearing.<P>If you idle for a while, the turbo keeps getting oiled, but has a chance to spin down.  Then when you shut off it has far less time needed to stop spinning with no fresh lube.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||10-25-2001||12:47 PM||||the cool down has nothing to do with RPM.. it's all heat. the if you kill a turbo car after hard running since the turbo is driven by heat and exhasut gas pulse the oil sits in the housing easily 1200*(f) and can over time cause build up which will lead to bearing/bushing or center shaft failure. Vortech, Paxton, Rotrex and such all have really high internal temps and all recommend the same procedure... even eaton recommends it on their units (roots type) Nitrous the only down side i've found so far is refilling. it sucks but for the $5000 it would run for a turbo or more for a V8  i can fill my bottles for over 2 years once a week. and still have money for dinner and $50 to bet aginst the guy next to me. <P>------------------<BR>0-60 in 4.91<P> <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A>  - underconstruction - but check it out anywho...<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Screwie||10-25-2001||12:56 PM||||What are centrifugal superchargers then? If those Vortech and so on are centrifugal superchargers, how come they have the same problem as a turbocharger?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Primaris||10-25-2001||01:05 PM||||150 bucks will buy you a turbo timer.  This is wired into the igniton system and allows you to lock your, car set the alarm, and walk away while you car idles and cools down the turbo.  A must for anyone who owns a turbo car.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Predator||10-25-2001||02:07 PM||||I like the idea of the timer. The only problem is I have a manual, and I don't want to have it driving itself down the road without me in it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I am too scared to just leave the emergency brake on and hope it holds.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Predator (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Turbo88||10-25-2001||03:01 PM||||You guys are right about the turbo's spooldown time. It's called coking and happens when the oil more or less burns onto and varnishes the bearings. While coking isn't RPM related, if you shut your engine down with the tubro spooled, it will eat your bearings quick. But if you are idling into a parking lot and shut it off, you won't have much problem. Like was mentioned above, a turbo timer will help eliminate that problem by allowing your turbo to spool down and cool off the turbine section. You can get a turbo timer for under 100 bucks USD. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||DKOV||10-25-2001||03:32 PM||||My only objection to what has been said above is that a Supercharger is not as efficient due to the fact that you may run out of RPMs before you get full boost.<P>That is simply not true.  There is a limit to the performance of a Supercharger just as there is a limit to a Turbocharger...<P>For example, a twin screw Supercharger delivers 85% of it's boost at 1800 RPMs and max boost by 4500.  From there on, you get only the max boost.  Of course, a twin screw delivers MUCH more HP than any other kind of charger.<P>My Powerdyne (centrifugal) Supercharger on my 2000 Durango R/T delivers its boost in a slower power curve giving only 50% of its boost by 2000 RPMs and max boost just under 5000 RPMs.  From there on, I get only 100%.<P>As with all chargers, the additional HP is created by the additional boost (air) created by the charger.  In my Powerdyne, I am only running 6psi which delivers me aroudn 110 (about 45%) additional HP.  A twin screw would deliver MUCH more boost at the same psi and as much as 85% more HP.<P>Of course, as stated above... HEAT is an issue   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Anyway, just so you know that I am not single sided...  My Lotus Esprit Turbo incorporates a Turbocharger.  This bad mamma boosts base HP from a 2.2 litlre FOUR cylider to 330HP!!!<P>Turbo lag is almost NON-existant with boost coming on around 2500 RPM and staying dead steady at max boost around 5000 - 6500 RPMs.  It pulls evenly through the entire bad right up to 7500 RPMs with no drop off at all.  The great thing is that when you are pushing the Lotus, each time you shift, it drops the RPMs right into the powerband!<P>Zero - 60 in 4 seconds!  That's the power of a Turbo!  Especially when you consider that an unpowered Lotus is a 4 banger.<P>Where does all this land in the bigger picture?  Well... Torbo chargind my Lotus peaks the HP at 330 HP...  My 3.4L TDC motor, tuned and tweaked... alot... lands at nearly 325 HP on paper (yet to be dyno'd).<P>The difference between these two cars is NOT MUCH except that one is normally aspirated.<P>The WEIGH nearly the same, with the suspension mods, they handle almost the same (Lotus IS better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) and the are made of the very same materials...<P>Which is better?  I don't really know.  My Fiero like to rip my head off just as easily as the Lotus except that with the Lotus, I get a half second to prepare for the boost.<P>Do you NEED a Turbo?  Perhaps.  Ask anyone that has a Normally Aspirated V8 tucked in the back if THEY need a charger or NOS...<P>Well... NEED and WANT are two entirely different things  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||10-25-2001||03:56 PM||||1) Eaton SCs do NOT suffer the drastic problems you get with Centrifugals (Vortec, Paxton...), such as<BR>a) no low boost<BR>b) too much boost to be useful in the top end<BR>c) MUST use engine oil<BR>d) MUST spin down.<BR>...since they do NOT use engine oil, and produce boost in relation to their displacement <B>not in relation to RPMs</B>. <P>2) They offer plently of LOW end boost, without overboosting at high RPMs. <P>3) They are usually utilized with a <B>bypass</B> system, so when you're not at more than 75% throttle, they do NOT produce boost, and (accordint to Eaton themselves) pull only 0.6HP to spin freely. This saved gas mileage, engine longevity, SC life, etc..<P>This is why I am an Eaton guy...all the way. It's the ONLY way to boost. <BR><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Turbo88||10-25-2001||05:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>1) Eaton SCs do NOT suffer the drastic problems you get with Centrifugals (Vortec, Paxton...), such as<BR>a) no low boost<BR>b) too much boost to be useful in the top end<BR>c) MUST use engine oil<BR>d) MUST spin down.<BR>...since they do NOT use engine oil, and produce boost in relation to their displacement [b]not in relation to RPMs</B>. <P>2) They offer plently of LOW end boost, without overboosting at high RPMs. <P>3) They are usually utilized with a <B>bypass</B> system, so when you're not at more than 75% throttle, they do NOT produce boost, and (accordint to Eaton themselves) pull only 0.6HP to spin freely. This saved gas mileage, engine longevity, SC life, etc..<P>This is why I am an Eaton guy...all the way. It's the ONLY way to boost. <P><BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>1.A - Actually, the centrifugal superchargers are more efficient than the roots, screw types. Generally speaking, centrifugal units use centrifical force and to a lesser degree, the Bernoulli Principal (venturi) to produce boost. They do compress the air but not like a roots/screw type compressor does. Because of this, they tend to add less heat which means they are more efficent. Centrifugal superchargers don't suffer from any more 'lag' than a roots type does. Turbos do to a certain degree although you might have to run it a lot on a dyno to find it.<P>1.B - Turbos don't preduce excessive boost at high end either because they allow some of the exhaust to bypass the turbine when the maximum preset amount of boost is achived. <P>1.C - This is true but it's a matter of taste here. I like the idea of using engine oil because it is easier to keep track of one lubrication system than two.<P>1.D - Again, true. But its not like you have to sit in the parking lot for 10 minutes each time you drive a turbo car somewhere. If you come into your parking spot at full throttle and shut the engine off, you will coke it eventually. Turbos require a little more for-thought on the part of the driver when it comes to this.<P>2-3. You are wrong about an Eaton being independant of RPM. The faster it's rotors spin, the more times the rotors will "open a vacuum" ontop of the supercharger in a given amount of time. That means it will compress more air more times and will ultimately push more air into the engine. Here is a quote from Eaton's website "An Eaton supercharger provides improved horsepower and torque, at lower engine rpm's, by pumping extra air into the engine in direct relationship to crankshaft speed". You are correct about the fact that displacement place into the equation. If your rotors and longer and wider, they will have more area and thus move more air at a given RPM. The same can be said about turbos and centrifugal units. <P>You also mentioned that it takes .6 horsepower to turn an Eaton. I'm afriad that is also incorrect. Maybe they mean .6hp per cfm? You did say "to spin freely" so I am assuming that you mean it take .6hp to spin it under no load (pumping no air). That might be correct on a small unit but why would you want a supercharger if you aren't going to move air with it?<P>The bypass system is very very poor when compaired to a wastegate system that a turbo uses. You are bypassing extra boost but you are still using engine horsepower to move that air. In contrast, a turbo allows some of the exhaust to skip the turbine entirely which means it isn't using energy it doesn't need. That means the fabiled exhaust restriction is less at full boost. Plus if you are still pumping air that is being bypassed, you are using still using engine horsepower which also means you are burning more gas, not less. <P>If you find that Eaton brand superchargers are a product that meat your needs, I say run with it. They make a good superchager. But they are far from the only way to boost..<P>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Turbo88||10-25-2001||05:54 PM||||Some very good points here. Hope you don't mind me adding some thoughts DKOV...<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B>My only objection to what has been said above is that a Supercharger is not as efficient due to the fact that you may run out of RPMs before you get full boost. <P>That is simply not true.  There is a limit to the performance of a Supercharger just as there is a limit to a Turbocharger...</B><P>Actually it is true. It depends on the gearing of the supercharger. If you reach max boost before redline, you will have to vent some of it off which means you are wasting engine horsepower compressing air you don't need compressed. <P><B>For example, a twin screw Supercharger delivers 85% of it's boost at 1800 RPMs and max boost by 4500.  From there on, you get only the max boost.  Of course, a twin screw delivers MUCH more HP than any other kind of charger.</B><P>I agree. The screw type spinoff of the roots supercharger is by far the best of it's type. If I recall NHRA banned them for use on the nitomethane cars. <P><B>My Powerdyne (centrifugal) Supercharger on my 2000 Durango R/T delivers its boost in a slower power curve giving only 50% of its boost by 2000 RPMs and max boost just under 5000 RPMs.  From there on, I get only 100%. </B><P>You get 100% plus   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P><B>As with all chargers, the additional HP is created by the additional boost (air) created by the charger.  In my Powerdyne, I am only running 6psi which delivers me aroudn 110 (about 45%) additional HP.  A twin screw would deliver MUCH more boost at the same psi and as much as 85% more HP.</B><P>I've always understood the term 'boost' to be a replacement for PSI. IE 7 pounds of boost = 7 psi. In anycase at the same airflow the screw beats the roots because it is more efficent which means cooler air which means denser air charge.<P><B>Of course, as stated above... HEAT is an issue     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> </B><P>Yes it can, yes it can. But you have to take that into account when you design your setup.<P><B>Anyway, just so you know that I am not single sided...  My Lotus Esprit Turbo incorporates a Turbocharger.  This bad mamma boosts base HP from a 2.2 litlre FOUR cylider to 330HP!!!<P>Turbo lag is almost NON-existant with boost coming on around 2500 RPM and staying dead steady at max boost around 5000 - 6500 RPMs.  It pulls evenly through the entire bad right up to 7500 RPMs with no drop off at all.  The great thing is that when you are pushing the Lotus, each time you shift, it drops the RPMs right into the powerband!<P>Zero - 60 in 4 seconds!  That's the power of a Turbo!  Especially when you consider that an unpowered Lotus is a 4 banger.<P>Where does all this land in the bigger picture?  Well... Torbo chargind my Lotus peaks the HP at 330 HP...  My 3.4L TDC motor, tuned and tweaked... alot... lands at nearly 325 HP on paper (yet to be dyno'd).<P>The difference between these two cars is NOT MUCH except that one is normally aspirated.<P>The WEIGH nearly the same, with the suspension mods, they handle almost the same (Lotus IS better    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) and the are made of the very same materials...<P>Which is better?  I don't really know.  My Fiero like to rip my head off just as easily as the Lotus except that with the Lotus, I get a half second to prepare for the boost.<P>Do you NEED a Turbo?  Perhaps.  Ask anyone that has a Normally Aspirated V8 tucked in the back if THEY need a charger or NOS...<P>Well... NEED and WANT are two entirely different things    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Lotus is a great car. I LOVE what they did with the LT5 in the ZR1 vette. That engine is a work of art.!!<p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||10-25-2001||08:00 PM||||I love my turbo V6. I pondered the V8 swaps, but decided i could keep everything else in engine bay pretty much factory. I only need to run 4-5 pnds boost to kick most cars butts and its still totally drivable any way i want to drive.||||reg||1
Z||000015||GTMike||10-27-2001||02:47 AM||||roger.  have you had your car dynoed??  do you have the 2.8? i'm thinking of makeing a turbo setup that runs around 5 or 6 psi for my 2.8   and just wondering how much horse power. i would get. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||TRiAD||10-27-2001||11:41 AM||||Turbo, <BR>On my point #1, I didn't say the Eaton was more "efficient" than the others, because it's not. I said it didn't suffer from lag (and Centrifugals DO have lag, I've driven MANY), and that it doesn't require engine oil, and that it does not "overboost" like Centrifugals. <P>Also, Eaton's website is where the 0.6HP came from, and if you don't know why "you wouldn't want to be boosting", read my fist post again. A system that boosts ALL the time is simply ignorant (IMO). <P>The bypass system is the way it is with an Eaton, instead of a stupid (IMO) wastegate, since with the wastegate, you're doing just that...WASTING boost. Like turbos and Centrifugals ALWAYS do. The Eaton system simply does NOT boost unless you WANT it to. If it used a wastegate instead of a bypass, it would take much more HP to spin it when you're not using it, and the excess boost would be released after the blower. With the bypass, it doesn't boost in the first place. Make sense?<P>Eatons have ALWAYS been utilized for their ability to produce a relatively FLAT torque curve; they produce most of their boost low in the RPM range, and continue to boost to redline. The do NOT, however, continue to provide more and more boost with RPMs to the extent that Centrifugals do. <P>So, <BR>1) Boost when you want, not all the time<BR>2) Virtually unnoticeable when not in boost<BR>3) Make only the boost you want, not 5xs more and leak the excess<BR>4) Better reliability and longevity, of the blower AND your engine<BR>5) Permanently lubricated<BR>6) NO spin down time<P>I'm sold...||||reg||1
Z||000017||Screwie||10-27-2001||01:18 PM||||Can anyone tell me the difference between a centrifugal and the other kind of supercharger? Or maybe a link to a site where I can find it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> Would be much appreciated, thanks!  (I know, I'm an irritating tech-newb, sorrrry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000018||87GTZ34||10-27-2001||02:11 PM||||A centrifugal pump 'flings' air out to the sides of the housing and an opening on the outside of the housing allows air to escape.  Water pumps for your car, house, irrigation system etc typically use a centrifugal method to pump water.  A 'roots type' blower has rollers (like dough rollers except not flat) that paddle the air and then paddles inter lock as they roll moving the air from the top to underneath.  The top has vacuum the bottom of the rollers has pressure.  The 'roots type' is called a positive displacement pump.  Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Screwie||10-27-2001||02:16 PM||||definitely! Thanks a lot!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||TRiAD||10-27-2001||02:18 PM||||Centrifugal; This is basically the "clean-air" side of a turbo, but driven by a bely, instead of the exhaust. It produces boost using the same physics as a turbo, and requires engine oil like a turbo and therefore needs to be "idled" ifter a hard run, just like a turbo. <P>Eaton; This is a modern re-design of the old "roots" type blower. Really, they have almost nothing in common with "roots" anymore, and are much more efficient than the roots are. This basically has 2 "screws" side by side, one has convex lobes, the other has concave lobes. They interlock and rotate together when driven by the belt. <P><B>Centrifugal</B> top end power only, "overboosts" to compensate for no low-end, and therefore make too much top end boost and MUST have a blow-off valve/wastegate to avoid blowing your engine to mars. Requires engine oil (tapping the oil pan, usually) and therefore must be idled down after a run, so you don't boil static oil into the bearings. <P><B>Eaton</B> low end power and top end, makes only the PSI of boost required for the application, and therefore does not need to make too much top end boost and does not need a blow-off valve/wastegate to avoid blowing your engine to mars. Does not require engine oil (tapping the oil pan, usually) and therefore does not need to be idled down after a run, so you don't boil static oil into the bearings.<P>As you can tell, I'm a big fan of the Eatons. The only downside they have is they generally are limited to 10-12PSI of power, but on the street, that's more than I'd ever need anyway. If you need more than that, get a bigger engine. <P>&lt;wow! 600 posts! whooeeey!&gt;<BR><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 10-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Turbo88||10-29-2001||01:43 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>Turbo, <BR>On my point #1, I didn't say the Eaton was more "efficient" than the others, because it's not. I said it didn't suffer from lag (and Centrifugals DO have lag, I've driven MANY), and that it doesn't require engine oil, and that it does not "overboost" like Centrifugals. <P>Also, Eaton's website is where the 0.6HP came from, and if you don't know why "you wouldn't want to be boosting", read my fist post again. A system that boosts ALL the time is simply ignorant (IMO). <P>The bypass system is the way it is with an Eaton, instead of a stupid (IMO) wastegate, since with the wastegate, you're doing just that...WASTING boost. Like turbos and Centrifugals ALWAYS do. The Eaton system simply does NOT boost unless you WANT it to. If it used a wastegate instead of a bypass, it would take much more HP to spin it when you're not using it, and the excess boost would be released after the blower. With the bypass, it doesn't boost in the first place. Make sense?<P>Eatons have ALWAYS been utilized for their ability to produce a relatively FLAT torque curve; they produce most of their boost low in the RPM range, and continue to boost to redline. The do NOT, however, continue to provide more and more boost with RPMs to the extent that Centrifugals do. <P>So, <BR>1) Boost when you want, not all the time<BR>2) Virtually unnoticeable when not in boost<BR>3) Make only the boost you want, not 5xs more and leak the excess<BR>4) Better reliability and longevity, of the blower AND your engine<BR>5) Permanently lubricated<BR>6) NO spin down time<P>I'm sold...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I posted my thoughts on this but, for some reason, when I posted it onle showed TRiAD's qoute.<P>The skinny of it is..<P>You are wrong about the wastegate vs bypass. A wastegate allows extra exhaust to skip the turbo thereby controlling boost. The Eaton system "wastes" boost your engine has spent horsepower creating. It vents it. You have the two almost completely backwards. <P>A centrifugal supercharger does not have any lag. They are belt driven just like an Eaton. Infact, given that they are more efficent, they offer better lowend boost than an Eaton unit does. <P>Eaton's almost always overboost but remove that overage by using it's bypass system. Centrifugal units which are geared for lowend boost do the same. Only a turbo's RPM can spin independantly of the engine's RPM because the turbo's driving force (the exhaust) can be made to go around the turbo and not spin it any faster. This is what a wastegate does. It doesn't have anything todo with venting clear pressurized air. <P><B><BR>If it used a wastegate instead of a bypass, it would take much more HP to spin it when you're not using it, and the excess boost would be released after the blower. With the bypass, it doesn't boost in the first place. Make sense?<BR></B><P>You are 100% backwards in the above statement. A wastegate allows exhaust to bypass the turbo thus not using any "horsepower" to spin the turbo. In an Eaton setup, the boost IS released after the blower. <P>I can accept that you like Eaton superchargers. They are a good product. But you seem to have no clear understanding of how either of them work. This isn't ment as an insult, it's just that you have been wrong in everything you posted above. ]   I would recommend exploring the web for some information on both systems. There is a wealth of information out there for the taking. I hope this helps clerify some missconception.<P>-Bryan<P><p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 10-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||TRiAD||10-29-2001||09:31 AM||||I'll only say this...<P>At NO POINT does the intake, once past the air filter, return to the atmosphere in an Eaton system, except after combustion. <P>You obviously need to research Eaton systems better before calling yourself an expert. <P><A HREF="http://www.jacksonracing.com/pages/howorksh.html" TARGET=_blank>Look at the diagram on this page...</A> There is NO blow-off/wastegate into the atmosphere...only a bypass that goes right back into the intake stream and allows the unit to run without producing boost. <P>Since you are so 100% completely wrong on this point, and defended your incorrect assumptions, I can only assume the remainder of your info is just as poorly founded. I hope anyone trying to take your advice considers their source carefully. No offense. <P>I am done with this thread...||||reg||1
Z||000023||RacinRob||10-29-2001||10:23 AM||||so are there any superchargers that will fit on a stock 6 cyc?  what about an ECU to run the engine under boost?  I know there is this stuff for turbos, but I haven't seen any for SC's other than the 3.8SC.... I think a super charger would be great... if there is the stuff out there to do it<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||TRiAD||10-29-2001||11:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RacinRob:<BR><B>so are there any superchargers that will fit on a stock 6 cyc?  what about an ECU to run the engine under boost?  I know there is this stuff for turbos, but I haven't seen any for SC's other than the 3.8SC.... I think a super charger would be great... if there is the stuff out there to do it</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Rob, I will be working on an Eaton system for the 2.8 shortly. It will be complete, and will run in 6 or 10psi (+ roughly 60-100HP+) for under $3,000...if I can keep it that low. So far, it looks good. <BR>It will include the charger (w/bypass, see diagram linked above), the manifold, the fuel system upgrade, all brackets, pulley and belt, and anything else necessary for install. <BR>Thanks!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||DKOV||10-29-2001||12:15 PM||||Turbo 88...<P>I should have stated "in a correctly designed Super Charger application"...<P>Thanks for the input!<P>Truthfully, the primary reason I went for the centrifugal Powerdyne Super Charger is the size and the "sleeper effect".<P>Most, if not all, super chargers make that whine that give them away.  Most, that is, excpet the Powerdyne.  It is totally quiet!<P>I love the fact that rice boy sitting next to my "Urban Assualt Vehicle" has no idea WHAT he is getting ready to "Beat"...  In fact, that may be my greatest joy in my Durango!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Suprisingly, no one wants to race me in my Lotus.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I don't get it.  Sure, I get the occasional Porche but I only seem to pick them up around 80 MPH and loose them are 125 MPH...  I wish they'd hang in there a bit longer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>My Fiero is the only normally aspirated car I own and I only get to let that loose every once in a while as well...<P>I'm only complaining because my on road racing seaon is over and now I have to wait until Sebring to let the throttles go again!<P>Bummer...<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000026||RacinRob||10-29-2001||10:46 PM||||If you get that supercharger kit put together, I'll be high on the list to get one.... I have to get another fiero.... But that is the easy part, the hard part is finding the one I want, anyone got an 85 se v-6 stick they want to let go of cheap?  I almost went and got an 87 gt till I looked at the wieght.  that kinda of power for that cheap I'm there..... If only I could afford to build a fiero that would beat my brother's 3000GT...... poor me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> anywayz keep me informed||||reg||1
Z||000027||lowCG||10-30-2001||11:28 PM||||After looking at what it'd take to make a different intake manifold for my TDC,I think I should just set-up a blower instead.<BR>Much more satisfying,and I think the Eaton can be adapted,once the fuel rail is out of the way a little.<BR>With the idle-boost-bypass feature,the Eaton unit should be plenty smooth.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Turbo88||10-31-2001||10:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>I'll only say this...<P>At NO POINT does the intake, once past the air filter, return to the atmosphere in an Eaton system, except after combustion. <P>You obviously need to research Eaton systems better before calling yourself an expert. <P><A HREF="http://www.jacksonracing.com/pages/howorksh.html" TARGET=_blank>Look at the diagram on this page...</A> There is NO blow-off/wastegate into the atmosphere...only a bypass that goes right back into the intake stream and allows the unit to run without producing boost. <P>Since you are so 100% completely wrong on this point, and defended your incorrect assumptions, I can only assume the remainder of your info is just as poorly founded. I hope anyone trying to take your advice considers their source carefully. No offense. <P>I am done with this thread...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm not going to get into a pissing contest with you. You have demonstated your lack of knowledge so many times in this single post, pathetic. I have never called myself an expert on Eaton superchargers but I have worked with them before.. Have you? You can't even get the terminology correct. The bypass system in the Eaton vents compressed air into the presupercharger air at atmospheric pressure. I never said it vents it into the engine bay and if I did, I'm wrong. In any case, it vents that pressure into unpressurized air where it must be RECOMPRESSED. That means that volume of air is compressed twice, using engine horsepower to compress it twice and it will be heated twice. <P>Also, I noticed a lack of response about your incorrect statements concerning wastegate system used by turbos. I have done my reasearch; what research I need to do. Once again, I never said Eatons are a bad product. I'm just not going to allow you to spread falsehoods about systems you fail to understand. Given your comment above about my advice, I would warn anyone considering the purchase of your engineering to think again. <P>Here is the source of my Eaton knowledge... <A HREF="http://www.eaton.com/supercharger/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eaton.com/supercharger/</A> <P>Notice it is the manufacture's website, not a reseller. <P>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||Turbo88||10-31-2001||10:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B>Turbo 88...<P>I should have stated "in a correctly designed Super Charger application"...<P>Thanks for the input!<P>Truthfully, the primary reason I went for the centrifugal Powerdyne Super Charger is the size and the "sleeper effect".<P>Most, if not all, super chargers make that whine that give them away.  Most, that is, excpet the Powerdyne.  It is totally quiet!<P>I love the fact that rice boy sitting next to my "Urban Assualt Vehicle" has no idea WHAT he is getting ready to "Beat"...  In fact, that may be my greatest joy in my Durango!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Suprisingly, no one wants to race me in my Lotus.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I don't get it.  Sure, I get the occasional Porche but I only seem to pick them up around 80 MPH and loose them are 125 MPH...  I wish they'd hang in there a bit longer   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>My Fiero is the only normally aspirated car I own and I only get to let that loose every once in a while as well...<P>I'm only complaining because my on road racing seaon is over and now I have to wait until Sebring to let the throttles go again!<P>Bummer...<P>DKOV -</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hear you on the road racing season, though the SCCA season runs into november here I believe. Our local track, Hallett, I think is going to run into novemeber aswell but I don't know when their local club season ends. I think they are having another high speed touring event before the year's end though. I want to finish and test the turbo kit on the track before then. Well see <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P>When I was stationed over in Germany, I ran a local associaction on Hockenhiem Ring. MY GOD THAT IS A FUN TRACK!!!!! I even had the pleasure of biting it in Sach's curve my first hot lap. I didn't have the Fiero then, I was running an FZR 1000. It would piss me off when these little 250CC Aprilas would zip underneigh me when I was draggn knee and footpeg. That's ok. You can't touch a liter bike down the start stop straight. <P>I think that Great Durango Rice Eater is a great idea! That is kind of what I had intended for the Formula. Very few of them would race my LS1 but they might try to 'pick on' a fiero! <P>I helped a guy put on a powerdyne on his 95 T/A. It ran damn good. I had come very very close to putting a Vortech G9 on my T/A. Newer mice motors do so well with the centrifugal unit. As soon as someone figures out how to put a rat motor in a fiero, then the fun begins... I'm sure it's already been attempted.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>Check this article out. This is is going to be my next Fiero project... j/k <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">... <A HREF="http://www.popularhotrodding.com/featured_article.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.popularhotrodding.com/featured_article.shtml</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||TRiAD||10-31-2001||10:30 AM||||OK dumbass, from that same site...<BR><I>"5) Bypass system used for unloading supercharger during idle and light load, resulting in better fuel economy and quiet operation..."</I><P>It can't "unload" the supercharger if it's still compressing air. The entire frickin reason for the bypass is that <B>the Eaton does NOT compress the air while in bypass</B>. <P>Call Eaton, ask them. You're no better than Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Turbo88||10-31-2001||11:37 AM||||      <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>OK dumbass, from that same site...<BR><I>"5) Bypass system used for unloading supercharger during idle and light load, resulting in better fuel economy and quiet operation..."</I><P>It can't "unload" the supercharger if it's still compressing air. The entire frickin reason for the bypass is that [b]the Eaton does NOT compress the air while in bypass</B>. <P>Call Eaton, ask them. You're no better than Ed.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>When are you going to stop making yourself look ignorant? I could sit here and say nothing and still win this argument with you. I've read the entire page. I know how they work. Take a second, step back and look at your Jackson Racing website. You will clearly see that the bypass is compressed air being decompressed into the presupercharger air stream at atmospheric pressure. Let me repeat, the compressed air is being vented into the air stream that is present at near atmospheric pressure. Now that we understand that the air is being compressed, then decompressed, then recompressed again lets talk about heat and how it relates to air density. It is being heated twice by compression. Have I lost you yet? Good. Now. What does all that mean? It takes engine horsepower to compress the air right? yes. Now we take some of that compressed air and let it vent into the atmosphere (intake air stream at atmospheric pressure). Now that air is almost completely decompressed. Right? Bypass means not blowing unneeded air into the engine? Good, you are still with me or am I giving you to much credit? So this compressed, then decompressed air is present in front of the superchargers rotors. When air flows through the superchargers rotors, it is compressed. Soooooo, if you take decompressed air allow it to vent back in front of the rotors it will recompress. That means that the air has been heated twice. You have twice used engine horsepower to compress the same volume of air. Congrats. You now have a basic understanding of the supercharger system you are trying to sell people. Would you like to enroll in turbochargers 101? <P>Let me repeat another point. I was correcting your numerous mistakes and falsehoods. I have said several times that Eaton makes a good product. I have also said that roots/screw blowers are less efficient that centrifugal types and turbochargers. You have yet to disprove this fact. My whole point in all of this mess has been that a turbo is a more efficient product especially for smaller displacement engines that don't have as much horsepower to spare. A 16-71 blower on a top fuel dragster takes more than 600hp to spin. When you are making over 5000hp that’s not a great deal but it still takes more than 10% of the available power just to spin the supercharger. Turbos do not, in any way shape or form use ENGINE HORSEPOWER to spin. They use heat, pressure, and gas speed to turn the compressor. Under certain conditions, that causes backpressure mostly at the low end. BUT, if you know anything about engines as you proclaim, you also know that a little more backpressure helps low end torque. That is why I have said repeatedly, a WELL DESIGNED turbo application will have next to no noticeable lag. You cannot rebut any of these points because A&gt; they are correct and B&gt; you don't know enough about what you are talking about to argue anything other than quoting a website whose sole purpose is to sell you a product. <P>You obviously do not have the technical knowledge to build anything as complicated as a forced induction kit and your primary weakness is all you want to do is try to blow yourself up and attempt to convince people that you know what you are talking about by memorizing buzz words and URLs. If you do make a kit, which I doubt you will ever do, I highly recommend a solid product liability insurance policy. <P>Thanks in advance for your education,<BR>-Bryan <P>To everyone else reading this post, I apologize for carrying it on. Normally I refrain from flame wars but TRiAD has so little understanding about forced induction systems and the things he has said obviously come from someone who has read one sales merchant's website and thinks he is the house expert on all of them. He doesn't even know what a wastegate is short of what I have told him. I would hate to see someone take such bad advice from someone like him and cost themselves a ton of hard earned money. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||TRiAD||10-31-2001||08:55 PM||||Since it's open to the "presupercharger air stream at atmospheric pressure", it never is compressed. It's not trapped between the SC and the cylinderhead, so it cannot be compressed. The air passes between the lobes of the SC, yes, but it cannot be compressed (and therefore is not heated) since it is not trapped. That's why Eaton themselves say that the charger only uses "0.6 HP of crankshaft HP to be freely spun while in bypass". <P>Now, go learn basic physics and get back to us. I could care less if you "read the whole page", you obviously don't "know how they work". <P>---------------<P>I love how when you run out of hard info, you revert to flaming me. I only used JRs website since they had an idiot-proof diagram, obviously you are more of an idiot than I gave you credit for. <P>You never called Eaton, either. <B>In bypass, the air is NOT compressed.</B> That's all there is to it. <BR>PS, you're really an as$hole, know that.<P>Cliff, if you're here, I think we all know this thread has long-since lost all usefulness. <P>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 10-31-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||Cliff Pennock||10-31-2001||09:16 PM||||I'm here, but I haven't read the thread. Is it worth closing?||||reg||1
Z||000034||TRiAD||10-31-2001||10:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>I'm here, but I haven't read the thread. Is it worth closing?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I would think so, it's just Turbo and I arguing now. The original poster has <I>plenty</I> to consider. I wouldn't delete it (f it were me), but I'd lock it. <P>I may be "over-moderating" however.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000035||Screwie||11-01-2001||04:51 AM||||Well, it's a shame you disrespect eachother. <BR>But anyway, my knowledge of these "forced induction" systems has tripled  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Thanks, you two (turbo88 and Triad). Next time, just try to respect eachother! (I think you both definitely know what you're talking about. Just a small problem with what's happening in the supercharger  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000036||Turbo88||11-01-2001||10:37 AM||||Cliff, I wouldn't close it. Debates between people bring out a lot of information for people to browse through. Up until the dumbass comment it has been friendly. I intend to keep it friendly. I don't disrespect TRiAD in so much as he has made his arguement; it's just what he has said has been incorrect. Now back to what correcting TRiAD...<P><B><BR>Since it's open to the "presupercharger air stream at atmospheric pressure", it never is compressed. It's not trapped between the SC and the cylinderhead, so it cannot be compressed. The air passes between the lobes of the SC, yes, but it cannot be compressed (and therefore is not heated) since it is not trapped. That's why Eaton themselves say that the charger only uses "0.6 HP of crankshaft HP to be freely spun while in bypass". <BR>Now, go learn basic physics and get back to us. I could care less if you "read the whole page", you obviously don't "know how they work". <BR></B><P>Once again, this is incorrect. Give you a simple science experiment. Get a balloon, and put a peice of scotch tape on it. Now poke a hole through the tape and the rubber with a needle. Now blow the balloon up. It still blows up right? Good, without letting the end loose, hold it for a while. After a while, it decompresses it. Excellent. Now you understand how the air in a Eaton is compressed and then decompressed. Even if the blower was spinning independantly with both the bottom and the bypass open, it is still compressing air. That is how it is able to move the air through the bypass and back into the presupercharger air stream! And whether or not it is bypassed it is still compressed and still heated. <P>----------------<P><B>I love how when you run out of hard info, you revert to flaming me. I only used JRs website since they had an idiot-proof diagram, obviously you are more of an idiot than I gave you credit for. <P>You never called Eaton, either. In bypass, the air is NOT compressed. That's all there is to it. <BR>PS, you're really an as$hole, know that.<BR></B><P>This is so typical of a TRiAD flame. I've never talked with him before this but I have read his other messages. Here is his basic train of thought...<P>I am right, I can't prove it, but I am<P>*someone challenges him*<P>I am going to go qoute some website I've read somewhere about a product I've never seen, installed or supported.<P>*someone challenges his points*<P>I am going to flame them, call them a dumbass and an ahole because I am right and they are wrong regardless of what anyone says. <P>You are right TRiAD, I am an ahole. I am so terrible that I took the time to offer a friendly debate on products and you spin it off into some, "you don't know what you are talking about, I am better yadayadayada". I have yet to insult you personally. Infact the only thing I have said that could be taken as an insult was "thanks in advance for your education". You have demonstrated your lack of maturity and knowledge. None of us were born knowing everything. I offered to present for both you and everyone on the board what I know from experience and research. I'm not out repeating what somenoe else said somewhere else. I think what has offended you more than anything is the fact that you are wrong, I pointed it out, and you don't like it. You started spouting off about this wastegate system and that bypass system and everything you've said have gone from not totally true to blatently false. I have offered more hard information than you have taken the time to try to understand. The difference between you and me is, you offer an arguement, I verify it's validity and offer my thoughts or rebuttal. You, on the other hand reword and keep repeating the same thing over and over. All that and you have STILL not been able to prove anything as far as your comments about turbos and wastegate systems. Sinse no one can talk with you about anything without you turning it into a flame war I'll offer this challenge...<P>Morton, IL is near Peoria if I remember correctly. I'll back up and give you more benefit of the doubt that you will ever deserve. You finish your supercharger kit, hop in I55 and cruise down to St Louis. I lived in Chesterfield until I changed jobs and moved to Tulsa. I was thinking, whether the powerfest works out or not, I'll meet you at the track right there off I55 as you're coming into St Louis. We'll have a friendly challange of engineering. I love bench racing as much as the next guy but we have come to a point where it's time to put our money where our mouth is. I believe the track runs through novemeber. You just meet me down there come Satarday between now and the end of november and we will see who is right and who is wrong. Here is the challange, I bet I'll smoke your Eaton kit on the same size engine and get better gas milage doing it. No flames, no crap, just a simple put up or shut up. What do you say? <P>-Bryan<p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||I'mSmall||11-01-2001||10:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B> The thing is...  the turbo can spin at up to 100,000 RPM (depending on model, bla bla).  If say you're out booting around and have thing spooled way up, then quicky shut the engine off, you also shut off the oil flow.  Screeeech, no lube to the turbo bearing.<P>If you idle for a while, the turbo keeps getting oiled, but has a chance to spin down.  Then when you shut off it has far less time needed to stop spinning with no fresh lube.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This guy knows what he is talking about. A turbo is lubed by your engine's oil. If you rev your turbo charged engine then shut it off it starves the bearings. This is the <BR>major cause of turbo lock up/ failure. I found this out at the orientation of my job, they explained to us all of the differences between turbos and SuperChargers(what I make for a living.) ||||reg||1
Z||000038||DKOV||11-01-2001||12:14 PM||||Hmmm...  I had always understood that the reason you idle your turbo before shuting the vehicle off is that the oil in the Turbo is super heated and if you let it sit there it will bake the bearings leaving carbon scoring.  This will in turn cause premature wearing of the bearings and failure.<P>Letting the car idle cycles the oil to pull the hot oil out and replace it with cooler oil as the system cycles it through the oil cooler.<P>When you idle your car for... my Lotus manual suggests 3 mintues... the Turbo spools down anyway.  To a certain level, I would think.  Looking at my boost gauge, if I'm "taxi-ing" my Lotus into the garage or bak to the pit after some time on the track, I'm not spooling the Turbo anyway.<P>What I am confused about, as if that's all  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> , is who in their right mind would switch off the ignition while UNDER BOOST?<P>Anyway, thanks for the imput!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000039||TRiAD||11-01-2001||12:49 PM||||Turbo, if we continue this, you need to shorten your posts. You're hurting my eyes, just like Ed used to.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, here's another experiment...<BR>Take an Eaton SC, place it on a stand, attatched to a cylinderhead with a throttle body on the other end (this is going to be a draw-throuh system). Don't forget the bypass. <BR>Now, crank the Eaton, and yes, you will produce boost between the SC and the cylinderhead. (That's the general point, right?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<BR>However, when you open the bypass, the air can escape back to the pre-supercharger stream, so it's not trapped. It passes through the lobes, <B>uncompressed</B>. It may circulate and pass through more than the twice you mentioned, although most should make it through the second trip, but the number of trips is meaningless. (There is no additional heat being produced as you claim, since there is no compression.)<BR>If you have a boost gauge on this system, it will show boost when the throttle is open and the bypass is closed, but when you let up on the throttle a bit and the bypass opens, you're boost gauge goes to 0. <P>I don't know how else to help you understand, I have even asked you to call Eaton. <BR>Seems to me on the bypass, you're the one not offering any solid proof, and only arguing "your experience". You won't call Eaton because you know they'll tell you <B>when their unit is in bypass, it does not produce boost</B>. <BR>Sure I may have your wastegate info messed up, I'll admit that, but I never said a few of the things you keep arguing with me. <P>1) I never said the Eaton was more efficient than the others, in fact, I specifically agreed with you that it is not. It not being as efficient does not mean the centrifugal works better at producing useable torque, it only means it is more efficient. <P>2) I never said I was an engineer. I will look into exactly what a wastegate is, but I must admit, I don't really care. Turbos in general have way too many drawbacks for me to get real excited about them. Now, there's about as many manufacturers using them as Eatons, so there are a lot of people who share your viewpoint, but I am not one of them. I do however, have a firm grasp of the Eaton system. Oh, and yes, I have had direct experience with Eatons. <P>Anyway, there's really nothing less to discuss until you call Eaton. Once you do, and they explain the bypass system to you, hopefully better than I can, you can post your result, and apology, if applicable. <P>Keeping it clean, <BR>~Michael||||reg||1
Z||000040||TRiAD||11-01-2001||01:08 PM||||Here's another description of what the bypass does. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.magnusonproducts.com/bypass_valve.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.magnusonproducts.com/bypass_valve.htm</A> <P>Yes, it's an Eaton dealer's webpage, but how does that matter?<P>I called Magnuson and spoke with Bill, in tech. (805) 642-8833<BR>He said when the bypass is open, the Eaton is NOT compressing the air. Air enters the throttle body and goes one of 2 places...<BR>1) It can bypass the Sc completely through the bypass valve and simply be drawn into the cylinderhead via vacuum, or<BR>2) It can pass through the SC, uncompressed, and enter the engine in the same condition (pressure and temp) as the air that never went through the SC. <P>...the open bypass circuit decompresses the air between the cylinderhead and the SC. Once that are is decompressed, and the bypass remains open, there is no compression happening. <P>Call him yourself. <P><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Turbo88||11-01-2001||03:28 PM||||Ok, here is what I did. I do have a complete understanding of the bypass system but, just to get a second opinion and make sure I haven't missed something, I called a friend at a local speedshop who deals with Eaton units. I explained our differenct points and here is what he said...<P>Any and all roots based superchargers move air by means of compression. When the rotors spin, they compress air between them. This is because, as the rotor spins, it creates a helical vortex that spins in the opposite direction of the other rotor. This creates a vacuum on the input side and pressure on the output side. They don't work like fans in that they don't create pressure waves. If you are familure with electronic, think of the intake plenum under the supercharger as an "air capacitor". It stores the air for use by the cylinders as they need it. Because the bypass outlet has an area smaller than the collective compression area of the rotors, it cannot eliminate the air with 100% efficiency. This is good because, as the engines air flow needs change and engine vacuum increases, it won't outpace the closing of the bypass valve and pull air through the bypass system. But it means that the air is always compressed through the supercharger. The air pressure in the plenum is a byproduct of the compression that occurs as it moves between the rotors. Compression is the only way a supercharger can move air. The reason it takes less horsepower in bypass is because of the fact that it doesn't have to increase the pressure as much. Boost doesn't have to mean positive manifold pressure. It can mean less negitive manifold pressure. Without a supercharger, an engine might pull -10 in/hg. In an Eaton bypass system, in bypass, it might have a manifold pressure of -4 in/hg. It's still adding air to the system, just not as much. But it is still compressing the air. It is still using engine horsepower, abeit not as much but it is still using engine horsepower and venting it into the air. I ran the .6 hp past him and he agreed that they were probably refering to .6hp per cubic foot of air. <P>This is the last time I am going to repeat this arguement. I have proven myself over and over again. I am correct. There is no argueing that. Just look at all the diagrams on Eaton's website. Call Eaton directly. Talk to them..<P>Now lets talk about all the horrible things about turbochargers. I'm curious how you can say that when you openly admit you don't know much about them?<P>You never did answer my challenge to settle this on the track. Sinse we both agree that this is friendly, as it should be, maybe you can help me orginize the powerfest *hint*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. Archie and WC Fiero said they would be there if the schedule doesn't conflict. <P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000042||TRiAD||11-01-2001||06:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B>...Any and all roots based superchargers move air by means of compression. When the rotors spin, they compress air between them.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Oh, that may be, but the air in the intake manifold is not compressed. This is what I have been saying. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...The air pressure in the plenum is a byproduct of the compression that occurs as it moves between the rotors. Compression is the only way a supercharger can move air.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you're right and the air is compressed between the lobes, that does not mean that the intake manifold is pressurized. Since the air can escape, you cannot create positive pressure there...it's just physics.<P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The reason it takes less horsepower in bypass is because of the fact that it doesn't have to increase the pressure as much. Boost doesn't have to mean positive manifold pressure. It can mean less negitive manifold pressure. Without a supercharger, an engine might pull -10 in/hg. In an Eaton bypass system, in bypass, it might have a manifold pressure of -4 in/hg. It's still adding air to the system, just not as much. But it is still compressing the air. It is still using engine horsepower, abeit not as much but it is still using engine horsepower and venting it into the air.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I see what you're saying here, but the fact is, the air is simply running through the thing here, it's not being "pushed" into the cylinderhead. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I ran the .6 hp past him and he agreed that they were probably refering to .6hp per cubic foot of air.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why did you call a buddy to prove your point and not the number I gave you? He's wrong, plain and simple. It's not boosting when in bypass, so there's no measuring the air to see what it's drawing. His info may be right for a Whipple, or other design, but Eaton is a LONG way from a "normal" roots blower. Eaton manufactures these units, they claim 0.6hp for some and "one third of 1 HP" for other units, nothing abouyt "per cubic foot"...Call the stinking number I gave you and end this. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>This is the last time I am going to repeat this arguement. I have proven myself over and over again. I am correct. There is no argueing that. Just look at all the diagrams on Eaton's website. Call Eaton directly. Talk to them.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What?! I DID. YOU call them and not some buddy of yours and see that you're wrong. This canNOT be settled until you call them. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Now lets talk about all the horrible things about turbochargers. I'm curious how you can say that when you openly admit you don't know much about them?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Gladly. <BR>1) They lag, period. Sure, you can use multiple and/or smaller turbos to lessen the effect, but you short shift into 3rd out of boost, and it WILL take a while to get back into boost. Eaton SCs do not lag. They are capable of producing boost at idle (if the throttle is open and they are not in bypass).<BR>2) No low end. Again smaller ones are better about this, but turbos, by design, are a "top-end" device. I'm looking for low-end torque. Eaton's deliver it. (same reason as above)<BR>3) Engine oil. OK, you must idle the vehicle and let them spin down and cool off while fresh engine oil is being forced through the bearings, or the turbo can be spinning at 100,000RPMs+ at VERY high temps, with no new oil coming in, and the old oil just "coking" onto the bearings. (this is why they sell "turbo-timers", they idle your vehicle for a specified amount of time with the ignition off, so you can go shopping or whatever) This causes poor reliability long term. An Eaton SC is self-sealed, and is considered by OE manufacturers to be a "100,000 mile part.  <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>You never did answer my challenge to settle this on the track. Sinse we both agree that this is friendly, as it should be, maybe you can help me orginize the powerfest *hint*   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. Archie and WC Fiero said they would be there if the schedule doesn't conflict.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I missed this, what was your idea again, and how would we keep it fair? Only a specific PSI allowed? I'm intrigued. I'll be testing 6 and 10psi, FYI. Where would it be?||||reg||1
Z||000043||grinthock||11-01-2001||06:48 PM||||I wanna get in on this Super talk..<P>Couldn't I take one of  these....<BR> <A HREF="http://www.magnusonproducts.com/m62_3rd_generation.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.magnusonproducts.com/m62_3rd_generation.htm</A> <P><BR>Cut a hole in the top of my manifold and somehow mcguyver it to work?  any ideas?<P>SOMEONE needs to make a eaton blower that replaces our plenum<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000044||baldlobo||11-01-2001||07:01 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B><BR>Any and all roots based superchargers move air by means of compression. When the rotors spin, they compress air between them. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well I hate to tell you and your friend is wrong Roots blowers don't do compress air(he must be thinking of screw which isn't what eatons' are) what so ever(itis done all inthe intake manifold), the air is moved around the outside of the blower case and down to the intake manifold(where it's compressed),not strait down the center and that is fact, he has no say in this; call the bloody number. Oh,yeah and one more thing you seem to be forgetting that on the other side of the blower there is something consuming the air, along with the small hole(bypass).<p>[This message has been edited by baldlobo (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000045||Mach10||11-01-2001||08:41 PM||||Why are you guys arguing??? You people are arguing semantics:<P>1) The Eaton's SC, while in Bypass does not waste boost. It's not producing positive cylinder pressure, so it <I>creates no boost</I><BR>Now, granted, it is using kinetic energy, even when "freewheeling" Take your balloon example, and cut a hole the size of the inlet, or a little bit smaller.<BR>Now blow it up again... Same thing. <BR>What Triad's been saying is that inherent in the Eaton design, is the fact that when not being used, it uses very little energy to keep it moving. Because the pressure gradient is the same on both sides of the impellers (due to bypass), the supercharger is only expending energy moving the air that's already IN the impelller. It doesn't have to "push" against anything, so the only resistance is from the air resistance within the blower, and from the contact surfaces... Triad's point is that this reduces the energy costs when not in use. Turbo is arguing that there IS an energy cost... Well YEAH. Making ANYTHING move has an energy cost, INCLUDING making a turbo impellor spin... Why are we arguing this?<P>2) Properly designed turbo systems will not have any appreciable lag. on LAUNCH Sure, you can see it on the dyno... But the Ass-o-meter will not tell you. But from personal experience, dumping a gear (1-3) and mashing it,  the car takes a considerable amount of time to spool up... The Celica will pull like a freight train in any gear... But you need to give it time to take a deep breath.<BR>Having said that, I whole-heartedly agree with the turbo = high-end statement. Yes, you COULD do a turbo design that gives you max torque at low RPMs... But it'd be easier to do that with a blower...<P>Now, BOTH of you, kiss, make up, and let us know who wins the race.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Personal comments: <BR>Turbo88: You know your **** ... Don't ruin the rep by ragging out Triad and his work. He's not really wrong, and neither are you. You guys are arguing about what constitutes boost, and power-consumptions when not performing any work...<P>Triad: You know your ****  too... Keep building that kit. The world needs more of 'em  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Don't get into a pissing match about Turbo vs SC. There is NO winners, only empty bladders  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000046||grinthock||11-01-2001||09:01 PM||||ANy takers on my question?<P>Has anyone made a blower that replaces our plenum, OR has anyone here taken one and "welded" or "connected" it to the top of the plenum by cutting/splicing.  You get the idea.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000047||Borgio||11-01-2001||10:28 PM||||I have a question. I'm thinking of putting a 3.4 in my fiero and turbo charging it. I plan to make one myself with the help of my dad, as opposed to buying a couple thousand dollar one. Got the idea from Dennis LaGrua's website. My question is, how much boost will be safe for my engine, using performance parts, or without? My dad is VERY conservative about everything and would only suggest about 5 pounds. But I want POWER.<P>------------------<BR>Jake||||reg||1
Z||000048||Mach10||11-01-2001||10:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Borgio:<BR><B>I have a question. I'm thinking of putting a 3.4 in my fiero and turbo charging it. I plan to make one myself with the help of my dad, as opposed to buying a couple thousand dollar one. Got the idea from Dennis LaGrua's website. My question is, how much boost will be safe for my engine, using performance parts, or without? My dad is VERY conservative about everything and would only suggest about 5 pounds. But I want POWER.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldn't recommend much more than 5lbs on a stock engine, with stock compression. 6 at the top end.<P>Any more, and expect to litter the road with parts. Don't go cheap on a turbo system unless you LIKE blowing engines  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000049||grinthock||11-01-2001||10:45 PM||||Race-Tech here was running a 9lb turbo, for LONG time, no problems, she got greedy and pumped it to 11 and blew a ring.<P>Totally stock engine, upgraded injectors and the like, but stock pistons, rings.||||reg||1
Z||000050||Mach10||11-02-2001||12:05 AM||||Sorry, should have been more clear. <P>6lbs of boost with minimal fuel system modifications might work. Any more, and you REALLY have to start worrying about fuel mixtures. The 2.8 injectors will not be able to keep up... Need to fix that.<BR>Might want an ECM change... AT LEAST a rising-rate fuel pressure regulator... It starts getting complicated. <P>Worth it in the long run, but don't EVER skimp out on the details  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000051||Borgio||11-02-2001||01:00 AM||||I'm aware of the replacing the injectors and I plan on getting a custom chip, and have it delay the spark slightly to prevent detonation. I will make all the modifications necessary to get the proper amount of fuel. I plan on doing a classy job, maybe even use forged pistons, crank, etc...Possibly even an intercooler. My dad said, "even performance parts break". He scares me, I DON'T want to break my engine.<P>------------------<BR>Jake||||reg||1
Z||000052||Mach10||11-02-2001||01:41 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Borgio:<BR><B>I'm aware of the replacing the injectors and I plan on getting a custom chip, and have it delay the spark slightly to prevent detonation. I will make all the modifications necessary to get the proper amount of fuel. I plan on doing a classy job, maybe even use forged pistons, crank, etc...Possibly even an intercooler. My dad said, "even performance parts break". He scares me, I DON'T want to break my engine.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You're going in the right direction. Drop the compression a little, and you can put more boost in... 12psi sounds about right... Go get some books... There's a good one out, but I can't remember the title... I'll poke around for it...<P>Edit: Found 'em:<P> <A HREF="http://www.magnumforceracing.com/store4/books_sa_design.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.magnumforceracing.com/store4/books_sa_design.htm</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000053||00lE||11-02-2001||03:51 AM||||       <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>ANy takers on my question?<P>Has anyone made a blower that replaces our plenum, OR has anyone here taken one and "welded" or "connected" it to the top of the plenum by cutting/splicing.  You get the idea.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There is already a Magnuson supercharger kit available for the newer 3400 SFI V6.  It would most likely work with just about any year 60 degree GM V6 motor that uses the GEN III aluminum heads.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blown3400.jpg">   <P>New or near new 3100/3400 motors can be obtained for less than $300.<BR>For example:<P><A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=594051363" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=594051363</A><P>or<P><A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=597128607" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=597128607</A><P>You can see for your self what the kit looks like here: <A HREF="http://www.partsforyourcar.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.partsforyourcar.com/</A> <P>Pretty expensive ($3,629.99) , but thats no surprise, the cost of the supercharger itself is quite expensive.  Here is the add that details the supercharger kit:<P><A HREF="http://www.partsforyourcar.com/Merchant2/merchant.mv?Screen=PROD&Store_Code=1&Product_Code=GA309&Category_Code=300[/URL" TARGET=_blank>http://www.partsforyourcar.com/Merchant2/merchant.mv?S    creen=PROD&Store_Code=1&Product_Code=GA309&Category_Code=300</A><P>Those cheapo DIY turbo installs seem very appealling when one considers the cost of the current turbo or supercharger "kit" offerings.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 11-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000054||TRiAD||11-02-2001||09:57 AM||||Grintlock; yeah, I'm going to be working on a kit once the Fiero gets here (Sunday). I have decided to use a different charger than the model I have here, so there will be a bit of time before it's finished, but I still want to have it tested to 10psi and running before April.<P>mach10; yeah, Turbo and I got ugly for a few posts, but wee straightened it up. We're still not on the same page, as you guys noticed, but's all good. <P>So, I hope to be offering 6 and 10psi kits for the 2.8 by April. I hope to keep them at or under $3K, with fuel enrichment, etc. <BR>I'm sure I'll post along the way, but I will definitely post once their done. ||||reg||1
Z||000055||Turbo88||11-02-2001||10:51 AM||||To many responses to respond to each one.  So I'll start here. After reading TRiADs response I think we have agreed on that point. The "buddy" I called has used Eaton units almost exclusively in the past. I trust him for an honest answer to my questions. I've never really trusted people I don't know at speedshops because, having worked at a few as a kid, and being friends with people who own them, I'll say this. Most of them will tell you exactly what you want to hear to sell a product. That is people you do not know. I did make an honest effort to call Eaton to verify everything. In any case, this discussion about bypass is a little off subject. <P>This started out as a 'which is better' conversation. So to get back to the subject, here goes....<P>For TRiAD's points here are my responses...<P>1) A poorly matched turbo will lag. A poorly matched anything will cause performance problems. You can do some simple things to reduce this problem. One thing to do if you have a stick is you can rev your engine hard as the lights are coming down the tree. That will spool the turbo and simple inertia will keep the impeller spinning. If you have an automatic, you should be stalling it anyway. Stalling a car to 2k will produce more exhaust gas flow than if you were driving at 2k because there is more load. It takes more fuel to keep the engine at that RPM. That to will help you spool your turbo in a drag race. Learn to do that correctly and you will be in positive boost on launch. Between shifts, a blowoff valve can be used to stop the reversing pressure wave from backing up to the impeller and slowing it down. One small displacement engines running low boost, that pressure wave is negligable. Because the turbo isn't connected directly to the engine, once it is spooled, it will stay spooled for a long time (long time from the engine's stand point). <BR>2) 100% correct. A turbo is designed to make power in the engine's operating range. Next time you go to the strip, make a run and measure the amount of time you are in the lower half of your engine's rpm range. With the exception of launch, your engine is always in the RPM band where the turbo is best utilized. Launch can be delt with per above. In a road race, if you are ever outside a turbo rpm band, you aren't driving hard enough. On the street, in my 88 in forth gear around 40 miles per hour, I'm running about 2500-3000 rpm give or take. That creates more than enough exhaust flow to create boost if I open the throttle body. If you are refering to lag here, you would need a very sensitive dyno to find it.<BR>3) If a turbo were spinning at 100k RPM all the time, then you wouldn't ever have any lag problem because the turbo would always make max boost. I learned more about the coking problem when I built a gas turbine engine out of a turbo then I ever delt with when was working on an automotive application. Without typing a thesis on turbocharger coking, I'll just say this. There is a reason almost every car that comes from the factory with forced induction comes with a turbo. <P>The powerfest I was talking about would be in St Louis, MO some weekend in the spring. The race I was talking about would be in a couple weeks. No limit to boost but it has to be a stock 2.8lt engine. I say stock because, if you are building a kit, as I am, that is what most of the kits will go on.<P>Everyone else had some good comments. Someone did say something about my reputation. I hope I didn't have much of one to begin with being relitively new to the board. With conversations like this, one can demonstrate book smarts but all to often people can't apply those "smarts". I hope everyone will consider me based on what I produce, not what I type in some computer. ||||reg||1
Z||000056||grinthock||11-02-2001||11:30 PM||||So someone didn't really comment on what I said.<P>Could you take a M90 Eaton the same as the 3.8's use and sort of weld it onto the plenum.  Ok maybe I am nuts, but has someone considered this?   Ok so let's take a M90 which exits on thebottom, cut the top of our plenum, and then weld it on in such positionthat it aligns with the belts..   What do ya all think?||||reg||1
Z||000057||TRiAD||11-02-2001||11:38 PM||||Turbo, on your points, we're obviously talking apples to oranges. You're talking 1/4 mile drags, in which on many street cars, yes, a turbo would be preferable (for this use only, not in daily use, IMO); I am however, talking about autocross, and daily driving. Now, you tell me how often you're at your upper RPMs in these situations. Daily driving; maybe 10%, if you're REAL hard on your car. Autocross? Once per run, at the 1-2 shift after your launch. <P>Looks like we agree to disagree here...you like turbos, you like 1/4 mile; I like SCs, I like AutoX. <P>Have a good one!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000058||Cooter||11-03-2001||12:13 AM||||I'm just hanging out waiting to see how Triad gets the Eaton mounted- I have run out of ideas on mounting mine, so I am very curious about how his kit will mount the blower. I have the ECM/boost/fuel/timing already addressed with the Syclone ECM, so all I need is a clue  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000059||Pontiaddict||11-03-2001||12:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>So someone didn't really comment on what I said.<P>Could you take a M90 Eaton the same as the 3.8's use and sort of weld it onto the plenum.  Ok maybe I am nuts, but has someone considered this?   Ok so let's take a M90 which exits on thebottom, cut the top of our plenum, and then weld it on in such positionthat it aligns with the belts..   What do ya all think?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blown3400.jpg"> <P>Look at the picture, it's not an M90, but the layout is still the same. The throttle body is connected to the rear of the s/c, then, hte outlet on what would be the bottom of the M90, is ducten to the intake. The only major differance here is that this SC is turned on it's side. <BR>The problem with putting the s/c on top of the intake is that it makes the intake too tall and it puts the pulley in a bad place. (You have to figure out where to adapt the serpentine pulley to the v-belt pulleys on the Fiero. Or maybe put a serpentine pulley setup on the 2.8)<BR>I don't know if the Grand Am setup will work as is because the supercharger looks like it wants to be where the firewall on a Fiero is. Maybe you can relocate the alternator and put the S/C on the other side of the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000060||grinthock||11-03-2001||01:33 AM||||That has been done, Randy O'Donahue of Haskell N.J. there was a aritcle in high performance pontiac Feb 92.  he did it on a GT years ago, looking through the BanditBalz Fiero library of articles (bandit has over 500 articles on the fiero), a guy removed his alt, mounted it on the other size, mounted the super on the left side with the alt, and instead of an alt onthe right side ran the belt on a pulley onto a ROD that ran on another pulley on the other side!  I know it's NUTS!  Sounds Nuts!  BUt the guy did it, and it worked. <P>But that's NOT what I want, I wanna use a roots blower, I looked at your pic, that blower is seperate or "adjunct" Iam talking on TOP of the series II 3.8 supers.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000061||Mach10||11-03-2001||03:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>Turbo, on your points, we're obviously talking apples to oranges. You're talking 1/4 mile drags, in which on many street cars, yes, a turbo would be preferable (for this use only, not in daily use, IMO); I am however, talking about autocross, and daily driving. Now, you tell me how often you're at your upper RPMs in these situations. Daily driving; maybe 10%, if you're REAL hard on your car. Autocross? Once per run, at the 1-2 shift after your launch. <P>Looks like we agree to disagree here...you like turbos, you like 1/4 mile; I like SCs, I like AutoX. <P>Have a good one!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I agree  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> For AutoX, you want low-end grunt, whipping out of the corners. No time to piss around waiting for the turbo to spool up... The few TC cars that were there did fairly well, but due more to driver skill, you could HEAR the damn things spool up, and SEE them START to get going JUST as they hit the next corner... D-Oh...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||10
